  "answer_text": "Great, here is a comprehensive overview of what can cause your bladder not to empty fully (a condition known as **urinary retention** or **incomplete bladder emptying**):\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udee7 Causes of Incomplete Bladder Emptying\n\n### 1. \ud83e\uddb4 Muscle Problems\nIncomplete bladder emptying occurs when the muscles of the bladder are not able to squeeze properly to empty the bladder. Specifically:\n\n- Pelvic floor muscle tension (hypertonic muscles) can restrict the natural flow of urine, while weakened pelvic floor muscles can't provide proper support for bladder function. Poor coordination between pelvic floor muscles and bladder muscles during urination, as well as weakness in the bladder muscle itself (the detrusor muscle), can also be causes.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\udde0 Nerve Damage or Neurological Conditions\nThis can happen if there has been nerve or muscle damage, perhaps caused by an injury, surgery, or disease such as Parkinson's disease, multiple sclerosis, and spina bifida.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udea7 Obstructions (Blockages)\nIn some cases, it can be due to an obstruction making it more difficult to empty your bladder. This obstruction can be caused by an enlarged prostate in men, a kidney stone blocking the urethra, constipation, or a stricture of the urethra, which makes it difficult for urine to flow out.\n\n- **In men specifically:** Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is the most common cause of urinary retention in males. The bladder is weakened when the prostate gland \u2014 which sits below the bladder and wraps around the urethra \u2014 is enlarged. This can cause you to lose the ability to empty the bladder completely.\n\n- **In women specifically:** Pelvic floor dysfunction due to genetics, age, obesity, or childbirth is a common cause of urinary retention in females. This may include a prolapsed bladder (cystocele), a common type of pelvic organ prolapse that can cause obstruction of urinary flow, where supportive tissues in the pelvic floor are weakened and can no longer support the bladder, causing it to drop into the vaginal canal.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udc8a Medications\nCertain medications taken for other conditions, including antihistamines, antispasmodics, opiates, and tricyclic antidepressants, can impact the nerves that signal the bladder muscle to contract, resulting in decreased function.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udebd Constipation\nPressure on the bladder from a large amount of stool in the colon can cause the bladder to not contract as it should or empty well.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83e\udda0 Infections\nUrinary tract infections (UTIs) and sexually transmitted infections (STIs) can cause inflammation and infection of the urinary tract and genitals, which can lead to urinary retention.\n\n---\n\n### 7. \ud83e\udea8 Kidney Stones & Other Structural Issues\nKidney stones crystallize, creating an obstruction that blocks the flow of urine. Large uterine fibroids may also press against one of the ureters, causing bladder issues.\n\n---\n\n### 8. \ud83c\udfe5 Catheter Use\nUrinary retention after catheter removal is common in both inpatient and outpatient treatments.\n\n---\n\n## \u26a0\ufe0f What Happens If Left Untreated?\nIf you are not able to empty completely, your bladder and its muscles may become floppy over time. With larger amounts of urine being held in the bladder all the time, urine will leak out when you don't want it to, and you may have a constant feeling of fullness.\n\nThere is also a risk that the residual urine that stays in the bladder will become infected.\n\n---\n\n> **\ud83d\udca1 Important:** If you are experiencing difficulty emptying your bladder, it is important to see a doctor. Many of these conditions are very treatable once the underlying cause is identified.",
